\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{M}{odern} data transmission infrastructures require cryptographic architectures that optimize execution throughput while mitigating physical vulnerabilities like differential power analysis. Standard mathematical frameworks, which rely heavily on discrete algebraic structures, frequently introduce latency penalties due to mandatory block-waiting queues and complex modular arithmetic. 

The Prism-Aetheris Substrate Encryption Engine (PASSEE) offers an alternative approach by transforming cryptography into a continuous-yield physical simulation. By representing discrete binary payloads as continuous phase-space wave-packets, the system uses the multi-dimensional physics of non-linear wave propagation to obscure underlying data configurations. When deployed over integrated silicon-photonic interconnects, this technique achieves deterministic, high-throughput scrambling directly at the physical hardware layer without traditional electronic-optical-electronic (OEO) conversion overhead.

\section{Mathematical Foundation \& Governing Equations}
The PASSEE computational substrate maps discrete classical bit vectors into continuous phase-space wavefunctions. The space-time evolution of an encrypted state vector $\Psi(\vec{r}, t)$ through the simulated refractive medium is governed by a generalized non-linear Schrödinger-Maxwell hybrid wave equation:

\begin{equation}
i \hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \Psi(\vec{r}, t) = \left[ -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m^*} \nabla^2 + V(\vec{r}) + \gamma \vert{}\Psi(\vec{r}, t)\vert{}^2 + \hat{H}_{\text{refract}}(\vec{r}, \omega) \right] \Psi(\vec{r}, t)
\end{equation}

Where $m^*$ represents the effective computational mass of the data envelope, $V(\vec{r})$ represents an arbitrary background masking potential, and $\gamma$ governs the self-phase modulation coefficient. The spatial tracking and scattering properties are isolated by the custom refractive token operator $\hat{H}_{\text{refract}}$:

\begin{equation}
\hat{H}_{\text{refract}} = \nabla \cdot \left( \frac{c}{n(\vec{r}, \omega)} \nabla \right) \oplus \left\{ \div, \circ, \bullet \right\} \otimes \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left( \mathbb{W} + \mathbb{Y} + \mathbb{L} + \mathbb{E} \right) \right\}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Refractive Index Tensor Formulations}
Data diffusion and spatial dispersion are dynamically updated by evaluating a high-index local refractive tensor matrix $n_{ij}(\vec{r}, t)$. This tensor incorporates linear refraction, localized intensity constraints, and third-order non-linear susceptibility terms ($\chi^{(3)}$) driven by the simulated optical fields $\mathbf{E}$:

\begin{equation}
n_{ij}(\vec{r}, t) = n_0 \delta_{ij} + n_2 \vert{}\Psi(\vec{r}, t)\vert{}^2 + \chi^{(3)} : \mathbf{E}_i \mathbf{E}_j
\end{equation}

\subsection{Non-Euclidean Curvature Space}
To prevent structural patterns from transferring to the ciphertext outputs, the spatial boundary layers are mapped onto non-flat topologies. Under these non-Euclidean conditions, the standard spatial Laplacian operator scales to accommodate the localized metric tensor $g_{ij}$, ensuring total Hamiltonian invariance $H$ across the coordinate space:

\begin{equation}
H = \int \left( \frac{\hbar^2}{2m^*} g^{ij} \partial_i \Psi^* \partial_j \Psi + V(\vec{r})\vert{}\Psi\vert{}^2 + \frac{\gamma}{2}\vert{}\Psi\vert{}^4 \right) \sqrt{\vert{}g\vert{}}\, d^3x
\end{equation}

The volume integration element is modulated by computing the metric determinant $\sqrt{\vert{}g\vert{}}$ across a four-dimensional toroidal hypersphere characterized by major radii ($R_1, R_2$) and minor rotational coordinates ($\theta, \phi$):

\begin{equation}
g_{ij} = \begin{pmatrix} r_1^2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & r_2^2 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & (R_1 + r_1 \cos\theta)^2 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & (R_2 + r_2 \cos\phi)^2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

