\section{Physical Implementation Framework (RPEC-1)}
Transitioning the PASSEE simulation engine into physical infrastructure is accomplished through the Refractive Photonic Encryption Core (RPEC-1). This physical-layer hardware interposer bonds a Tensor Processing Unit (TPU) die to a Photonic Integrated Circuit (PIC) using micro-bump copper pillars.

\subsection{Mach-Zehnder Interferometer Mesh Decompositions}
Arbitrary optical coordinate matrices are translated into physical hardware routing by processing the transfer matrix $T$ of an $N \times N$ Mach-Zehnder Interferometer (MZI) array. This lattice is broken down into a cascaded sequence of discrete beam splitters and phase-shifting loops:

\begin{equation}
T = D \prod_{k=1}^{N-1} \left( \prod_{j=1}^{k} T_{j, j+1}(\theta_{j}, \phi_{j}) \right)
\end{equation}

The mathematical behavior of each individual MZI node within the array is dictated by a $2 \times 2$ unitary transformation matrix:

\begin{equation}
U(\theta, \phi) = e^{i\phi} \begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta & i\sin\theta \\ i\sin\theta & \cos\theta \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Active Polarization Compensation}
To maintain signal stability across high-speed single-mode fiber links (SMF-28), inline polarimeters continually measure output Stokes vectors. A gradient-descent steepest-descent optimization loop monitors these inputs to adjust thermo-optic driver voltages ($V_x, V_y$) on a 50~$\mu$s clock interval, actively counteracting environmental birefringence.

\section{Empirical Validation \& Testing Results}
Validation testing was conducted using a $4096 \times 4096$ spatial collocation grid with sub-femtosecond temporal increments ($\Delta t = 10^{-17}$~s) to analyze the framework's behavior under severe operational conditions.

\subsection{Energy Conservation and Invariance Profiles}
Throughout long-duration test runs exceeding $10^8$ processing iterations, energy verification audits confirmed that total Hamiltonian invariance was strictly preserved within an acceptable variance window:

\begin{equation}
\frac{\Delta H}{H} < 1.2 \times 10^{-14}
\end{equation}

When processing inputs at the critical operational scale of 44.8~$\mu$J, high-amplitude solitons caused phase-slippage at the system's edge nodes. An autonomous 14~ms tensor dampening mechanism successfully contained these transient anomalies, restoring phase-space stability without data degradation or packet loss.

